import PachangaDiagram from "../../../../components/PachangaDiagram.astro";
---
  <h2 id="resumen">Executive summary</h2>
  <p>
    Pachanga Community organises a group's football matches: calendar, teams,
    statistics and community in one place, instead of scattered across a chat. The
    app is published on the App Store and Play Store, and the website serves as a
    shop window, the host for universal links and a support channel.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is no team behind it: product, design, app, website, infrastructure and
    support are all the same person. I have written very little code: 95% of it was
    written by LaIA, which is what I call my use of LLMs and agents. I have been
    playing Tony Stark, delegating as much as I can and still struggling to keep up,
    because many pieces have to work at once —infrastructure, cloud, web, stores,
    revenue, monitoring, PostHog and more— to give the user a careful, elegant,
    simple and direct experience that solves their problem. Everything runs on
    optimistic updates and on guiding the user by anticipating the action they want
    to take.
  </p>
  <p>
    The idea dates from 2019; back then I lacked the knowledge to take it all the way.
    In January 2026 I picked it up again from scratch, published it in the stores and
    now work on distribution and revenue while continuing to add features.
  </p>

  <h2 id="contexto">Context</h2>
  <p>
    In a group of friends who play every week, organisation lives in a chat: who
    plays, who cancels at the last minute, how teams are split and who keeps score.
    When the group grows, the chat stops being a tool and becomes the problem.
  </p>
  <p>
    The product attacks that: an app for the group, with privacy as a design
    decision. Changes are saved on the phone and synced with the cloud; there are no
    indexed public profiles and no data selling.
  </p>

  <h2 id="problema">The problem</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Coordinate a group that changes every week: call-ups, absences and balanced
      teams.
    </li>
    <li>
      Keep statistics people want to look at, without turning it into a professional
      league.
    </li>
    <li>
      Work with poor coverage on a football pitch, without depending on the network
      for every screen.
    </li>
    <li>
      Publish in two stores, in three languages and with no support team behind it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="restricciones">Constraints</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>One person.</strong> Every architecture decision has to reduce the cost
      of maintaining it six months later.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Two stores.</strong> App Store and Play Store with their reviews, builds
      and universal links.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Three languages.</strong> Spanish, English and Catalan, in both the app
      and the website.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Infrastructure cost.</strong> A cheap and reproducible self-hosted
      deployment, not a managed platform for convenience.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="rol">My role</h2>
  <p>
    Everything. Architecture, domain, mobile app, website, identity design,
    deployment, stores and support. What follows describes the decisions, not a
    division of work.
  </p>

  <h2 id="movil">Mobile architecture: hexagonal over DDD</h2>
  <p>
    The app is React Native with Expo and TypeScript. The structure is hexagonal over
    DDD: the flow goes from the screen to domain hooks, from there to use cases and
    from there to ports, which are implemented by Supabase or in-memory adapters.
  </p>
  <p>
    The domain layer is just TypeScript, without React Native, Expo or a database:
    the types and one line of Zod validation control the data that enters, leaves
    and moves inside the app. Use cases are tested without infrastructure, and
    changing provider means writing another adapter. The interface is organised with
    Atomic Design —atoms, molecules, organisms, layouts and screens— so a visual
    change does not force changes in the logic.
  </p>

  <PachangaDiagram />

  <p class="diagram-alt">
    In text: the app and its screens call domain hooks; hooks call use cases; use
    cases depend on ports; adapters implement those ports against Supabase, memory or
    cache. The interface is composed of particles, atoms, molecules, organisms,
    layouts and screens.
  </p>

  <h2 id="dominio">A domain that is not a CRUD</h2>
  <p>
    The model has team, season, match and player, but the interesting part is at the
    edges. A player belongs to a team with their own name and attributes; matches
    allow ghost players who later claim their profile with a one-time code; ratings
    between teammates calculate a relative level within the group, not an absolute
    score.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each of those rules has a use case and its tests. It is the part of the project I
    have reused most: when the domain is isolated, the app can change shape without
    the business noticing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="web">Web architecture: Ghost, generated theme and three languages</h2>
  <p>
    The website is Ghost with a custom Handlebars theme. The theme is not edited by
    hand: it is generated from its own sources —templates and translation files— and
    the build writes the variants for each language. A new page in three languages
    therefore does not mean three templates drifting apart.
  </p>
  <p>
    Besides being a shop window, the website hosts universal links and is a
    discovery surface: support, privacy and terms routes per language, an
    `llms.txt`, a catalogue for agents and a `robots.txt` with a content signal.
  </p>

  <h2 id="enlaces">Universal links and fallback</h2>
  <p>
    A link shared in a chat has to open the app if it is installed and take you to the
    store if it is not. That is two pieces: `apple-app-site-association` for iOS and
    `assetlinks.json` for Android, served by its own nginx; and a web fallback that
    redirects to the store when the link reaches a mobile browser.
  </p>
  <p>
    The awkward case is the claim link for a ghost player: if the person opening it
    does not have the app, they cannot be left on an error page. That is why the
    localised 404 also redirects to the store, instead of leaving the user in a dead
    end.
  </p>

  <h2 id="despliegue">Deployment and operations</h2>
  <p>
    Locally, everything starts with Docker Compose. In production, the website runs
    on a stack with Traefik over Dokploy, with the Ghost container, the database and
    the nginx for universal links. The app is built and published with EAS and
    Fastlane for both stores.
  </p>
  <p>
    None of that is glamorous, but it is what makes the product exist: certificates,
    routes, Traefik priorities, environment variables and a build that fails early
    if a configuration file is not where it should be.
  </p>

  <h2 id="harness">How the code is written: the agent harness</h2>
  <p>
    A good part of Pachanga's code is written by agents. They implement and test; I
    define the problem, review and decide. The harness is what makes that division
    work: `.instructions` files that say how each piece is written, Biome plugins
    that stop what should never reach review, and deterministic scripts that check
    what a visual review cannot see.
  </p>
  <p>
    I am not trying to write more code faster. I am trying to make the agent write
    better: the same criteria across every layer, fewer repeated decisions and more
    of my time on what moves the business. When the pattern is the same from top to
    bottom, review stops being archaeology.
  </p>

  <h2 id="decisiones">Technical decisions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Hexagonal over DDD on mobile.</strong> Pure domain and interchangeable
      adapters, in exchange for more files per feature.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Local-first with Supabase.</strong> The app works without a network, in
      exchange for assuming sync and conflicts.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Generated web theme.</strong> One source for three languages, in
      exchange for a build step you have to understand.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Own infrastructure on Dokploy.</strong> Control and low cost, in
      exchange for operating it myself.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="resultados">Outcomes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>App published on the App Store and Play Store, with its website and support channel.</li>
    <li>
      A domain tested without infrastructure, with adapters for Supabase and memory.
    </li>
    <li>
      Three languages in app and website from a single copy source per page.
    </li>
    <li>
      Links that open the app when installed and take you to the store when not.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="aprendizajes">Lessons learned</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Owning the whole product changes your judgement: every piece has to be obvious
      to the version of you six months from now.
    </li>
    <li>
      I like making the complex simple. When the whole project follows the same
      pattern with clear rules —enum → entity → port → use case, with hooks injecting
      the adapter that implements the port— knowing where each piece lives is fast. In
      Pachanga I have changed adapters fairly often, reimplementing them so the e2e,
      unit and mock tests passed; without that architecture, every change would have
      crossed the entire app. It is dependency injection, the backend kind, brought to
      the frontend. Along with the Biome plugins and deterministic scripts, it is one
      of the pieces of the project's agent harness.
    </li>
    <li>
      Universal links, stores and deployment are invisible to everyone, and they
      decide whether the product is finished or merely published.
    </li>
  </ul>
